    "text": "To solve the problem, we will use the given information to set up equations and solve for the values of \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\).\n\nFirst, we know that the arithmetic mean of \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) is 10. This can be written as:\n\\[\n\\frac{A + B + C}{3} = 10\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by 3, we get:\n\\[\nA + B + C = 30\n\\]\n\nNext, we are given that the value of \\(A\\) is six less than the value of \\(B\\). This can be written as:\n\\[\nA = B - 6\n\\]\n\nWe are also given that the value of \\(C\\) is three more than the value of \\(B\\). This can be written as:\n\\[\nC = B + 3\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) in terms of \\(B\\) into the equation \\(A + B + C = 30\\):\n\\[\n(B - 6) + B + (B + 3) = 30\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\\[\nB - 6 + B + B + 3 = 30\n\\]\n\\[\n3B - 3 = 30\n\\]\n\nAdd 3 to both sides to isolate the term with \\(B\\):\n\\[\n3B = 33\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 3 to solve for \\(B\\):\n\\[\nB = 11\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(B\\), we can find \\(A\\) and \\(C\\):\n\\[\nA = B - 6 = 11 - 6 = 5\n\\]\n\\[\nC = B + 3 = 11 + 3 = 14\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(C\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}\n\\]",
